 ## Statement
 
-Let $f$ be a smooth autonomous vector field on an open subset of $\mathbb R^d$. Let $x_*(t)$ be a nonconstant periodic solution of least period $T$, let $\Gamma=\{x_*(t):0\le t<T\}$, and let $\Sigma$ be a local smooth Poincare section through $a=x_*(0)$ transverse to $f(a)$. Assume locally $\Sigma=\{y:\rho(y)=0\}$ for a smooth $\rho$ with $D\rho(a)f(a)\ne0$, and take a relatively compact section patch $W$ with $\overline W\cap\Gamma=\{a\}$. If the derivative of the local return map $P$ at $a$ has no eigenvalue on the unit circle, then $\Gamma$ is orbitally asymptotically stable when all eigenvalues of $DP(a)$ have modulus less than one, and is orbitally unstable when at least one has modulus greater than one.
+Let $f$ be a $C^1$ autonomous vector field on an open subset of $\mathbb R^d$. Let $x_*(t)$ be a nonconstant periodic solution of least period $T$, let $\Gamma=\{x_*(t):0\le t<T\}$, and let $\Sigma$ be a local smooth Poincare section through $a=x_*(0)$ transverse to $f(a)$. Assume locally $\Sigma=\{y:\rho(y)=0\}$ for a smooth $\rho$ with $D\rho(a)f(a)\ne0$, and take a relatively compact section patch $W$ with $\overline W\cap\Gamma=\{a\}$. If the derivative of the local return map $P$ at $a$ has no eigenvalue on the unit circle, then $\Gamma$ is orbitally asymptotically stable when all eigenvalues of $DP(a)$ have modulus less than one, and is orbitally unstable when at least one has modulus greater than one.
 
